1
00:00:05,146 --> 00:00:14,412
[SPEAKER_00]: So, here are the few excerpts from Manjumanikāya, and mostly it's from Mūla Pāryāya Sutta, the number one.

2
00:00:19,335 --> 00:00:28,120
[SPEAKER_00]: This is on a difference between Putujana, Ārya Sāvaka, and an arahant conceiving.

3
00:00:30,702 --> 00:00:51,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, because an untaught ordinary person who does not see the noble ones, who is unskilled and untrained in noble ones' dhamma, who does not see good men, who is unskilled and untrained in good men's dhamma, from earth he perceives earth.

4
00:00:53,666 --> 00:01:03,648
[SPEAKER_00]: Having perceived earth from earth, he conceives earth, he conceives in earth, he conceives from earth, he conceives earth as mine, he delights in earth.

5
00:01:04,048 --> 00:01:04,788
[SPEAKER_00]: For what reason?

6
00:01:05,528 --> 00:01:10,369
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he has not fully understood it, I say.

7
00:01:10,449 --> 00:01:22,272
[SPEAKER_00]: So, here, obviously, earth, the element of earth, well, pretty much can stand for anything, any phenomena that arises in the experience of Bhutujana through his senses.

8
00:01:23,130 --> 00:01:35,668
[SPEAKER_00]: So, anything that's perceivable, anything that's perceivable can be discerned back down to the fundamental presence of the four elements, one among them or so on.

9
00:01:37,152 --> 00:01:52,221
[SPEAKER_00]: So, having perceived sight from sight, he conceives his sight, he conceives in sight, he conceives from sight, he conceives sight as mine, he delights in sight, because he hasn't fully understood it.

10
00:01:53,101 --> 00:02:02,107
[SPEAKER_00]: So, on that very fundamental level of perception, of putrujana's perception of his sight, sound, smell, taste, touches,

11
00:02:03,120 --> 00:02:05,561
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already where he conceives it.

12
00:02:05,701 --> 00:02:07,122
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where, where the mistake is.

13
00:02:07,682 --> 00:02:11,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, this is not, this is not a momentary thing.

14
00:02:12,425 --> 00:02:17,688
[SPEAKER_00]: A sight of puthujjana does not last only for a split second.

15
00:02:18,048 --> 00:02:19,289
[SPEAKER_00]: A sight is an experience.

16
00:02:19,329 --> 00:02:21,130
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a, it's a phenomenological experience.

17
00:02:21,190 --> 00:02:25,192
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you have a sight of something agreeable, of something disagreeable.

18
00:02:25,712 --> 00:02:30,215
[SPEAKER_00]: You've seen something agreeable and disagreeable, and now your mind revolves around it, for example.

19
00:02:30,255 --> 00:02:32,476
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that, that sight is present throughout.

20
00:02:33,630 --> 00:02:35,551
[SPEAKER_00]: For the whole time, it endures.

21
00:02:35,611 --> 00:02:43,116
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha would say, it's changing while standing, the same.

22
00:02:43,456 --> 00:02:53,302
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not that Bhutujana must uproot this subconscious momentary flux of his consciousness and sights and whatnot.

23
00:02:54,153 --> 00:03:02,395
[SPEAKER_00]: He needs to... needs to understand the nature of the seen, the nature of the heard, the nature of the sense in any other way.

24
00:03:03,035 --> 00:03:05,075
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's... that's the famous bhagya-sutta.

25
00:03:05,135 --> 00:03:07,976
[SPEAKER_00]: In seen, there will be just the seen, when it's fully understood.

26
00:03:08,696 --> 00:03:19,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, when it's not fully understood, he conceives the seen, he conceives in the seen, from the seen, he conceives seen as mine, and he delights in it.

27
00:03:20,650 --> 00:03:22,251
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is no understanding of it.

28
00:03:22,911 --> 00:03:36,738
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's another... another reason to be wary of... of meditation practices that sort of... make people jump right into sort of like observation of sensations and so on.

29
00:03:36,838 --> 00:03:45,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowingly, like for Pūtūjana, because if Pūtūjana spends time with his observation, he's spending time with his own conceivings.

30
00:03:46,787 --> 00:03:55,532
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, just perceiving things through your senses cannot reveal you the truth, because that's exactly the problem here.

31
00:03:56,193 --> 00:04:01,716
[SPEAKER_00]: Pūtūjana is just perceiving earth as earth, but because of that, he conceives it.

32
00:04:02,617 --> 00:04:05,098
[SPEAKER_00]: His perception is not full understanding.

33
00:04:05,759 --> 00:04:11,983
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what Pūtūjana perceives cannot undo his state of Pūtūjana.

34
00:04:12,969 --> 00:04:17,472
[SPEAKER_00]: Only what Putujana understands can undo his state of Putujana.

35
00:04:19,233 --> 00:04:30,161
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if a meditation technique or practice is about just perceiving sensations in your body, in your feet and so on, that has nothing to do with understanding.

36
00:04:30,781 --> 00:04:35,544
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you are perceiving things ignorantly with your ignorance, basically.

37
00:04:35,664 --> 00:04:36,565
[SPEAKER_00]: It comes down to that.

38
00:04:36,825 --> 00:04:41,068
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, perception cannot free a Putujana.

39
00:04:42,907 --> 00:04:44,827
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter what... even he perceives nibbāna.

40
00:04:44,867 --> 00:04:46,468
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, there are the suttas that say that.

41
00:04:47,608 --> 00:04:48,548
[SPEAKER_00]: He perceives nibbāna.

42
00:04:48,588 --> 00:04:50,488
[SPEAKER_00]: He thinks this nibbāna is mine and so on.

43
00:04:50,928 --> 00:04:52,269
[SPEAKER_00]: And Buddha said, yeah, that is nibbāna.

44
00:04:52,829 --> 00:04:55,149
[SPEAKER_00]: But because he doesn't understand it, he doesn't attain it.

45
00:04:55,229 --> 00:04:56,469
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's about understanding.

46
00:04:56,789 --> 00:05:03,090
[SPEAKER_00]: It's about understanding of the... of the five senses, the nature of it, the manifestation of the sight, sound, smell, taste, touches.

47
00:05:04,771 --> 00:05:11,712
[SPEAKER_00]: Not about perceiving the... the microscopic little sort of quick moments and catching them in their flux.

48
00:05:14,162 --> 00:05:20,789
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter what Putujana perceives, his ignorance is there with that perception, because it's in the perception.

49
00:05:21,470 --> 00:05:25,834
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why this sutta is called the root of all things, Mūla Pāryāya Sutta.

50
00:05:25,854 --> 00:05:29,818
[SPEAKER_00]: It means, no matter how far

51
00:05:30,700 --> 00:05:36,061
[SPEAKER_00]: Bhūtujana's perceptions kind of ranges how far it goes, how subtle it becomes.

52
00:05:36,882 --> 00:05:41,643
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot go below earth, water, fire and air as the four fundamental elements.

53
00:05:42,383 --> 00:05:45,284
[SPEAKER_00]: And even if he were to perceive them, he will be conceiving them.

54
00:05:46,104 --> 00:05:47,965
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the problem is not in the content.

55
00:05:48,045 --> 00:05:49,625
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not in earth, water, fire, air.

56
00:05:49,665 --> 00:05:53,286
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not in the sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, thoughts, for that matter.

57
00:05:53,746 --> 00:05:56,767
[SPEAKER_00]: The problem is in his perception.

58
00:05:58,372 --> 00:05:59,352
[SPEAKER_00]: It hasn't been understood.

59
00:05:59,613 --> 00:06:01,013
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that, it conceives.

60
00:06:02,854 --> 00:06:08,677
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the fundamental difference between Putujana and Ārya-sāvaka.

61
00:06:09,237 --> 00:06:18,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Says Bhikkhu, who is a trainee, who is Ārya-sāvaka, who still dwells aspiring for the unsurpassed security from bondage.

62
00:06:19,202 --> 00:06:21,923
[SPEAKER_00]: From earth, he directly knows earth.

63
00:06:24,204 --> 00:06:26,786
[SPEAKER_00]: Which means, that's the fundamental difference.

64
00:06:27,535 --> 00:06:38,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Satipaṭṭhāna, or higher, has understood, has understood the nature of things, has understood the nature of suffering, has understood the nature of perception, has understood the nature of earth, water, fire and air.

65
00:06:38,903 --> 00:06:41,384
[SPEAKER_00]: He's understood the nature of the appearing phenomena.

66
00:06:42,645 --> 00:06:45,267
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that, he's free from from suffering.

67
00:06:45,287 --> 00:06:46,708
[SPEAKER_00]: The Four Noble Truths are understood.

68
00:06:46,728 --> 00:06:50,450
[SPEAKER_00]: By knowing the way out of suffering, you are free from suffering.

69
00:06:51,011 --> 00:06:53,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Of course, an ārya-sāvaka,

70
00:06:54,843 --> 00:07:07,664
[SPEAKER_00]: As to stop now, or rather, make the effort of renunciation in regard to the habits that still linger from his previous ignorance, that now has been destroyed,

71
00:07:08,578 --> 00:07:11,499
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he's encouraged to not conceive Earth.

72
00:07:11,639 --> 00:07:13,980
[SPEAKER_00]: He's encouraged to not conceive in Earth.

73
00:07:14,500 --> 00:07:19,261
[SPEAKER_00]: He's encouraged to not conceive from the Earth, and he's encouraged to not conceive Earth as mine.

74
00:07:19,882 --> 00:07:30,085
[SPEAKER_00]: And that encouragement can work for him, because he has that reference point of having understood the Earth, directly knows the Earth, which is what's in common with Ārya-sāvaka and Arahant.

75
00:07:31,477 --> 00:07:55,346
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for as long as he doesn't... for as long as he, eh... fully doesn't develop the not conceiving in earth, from earth, earth is mine, for as long as he doesn't fully surmount the delighting in earth, inside sounds, smells, tastes, touches, he will, eh... he will still be subjected to... to the leftover symptoms of his previous illness of being a putujana.

76
00:07:55,586 --> 00:07:56,947
[SPEAKER_00]: But the illness is uprooted.

77
00:07:58,602 --> 00:08:14,154
[SPEAKER_00]: Even if he doesn't necessarily make much effort in this life, as the Buddha said, at seven most times, seven most occurrences of his lives, the work will be seen through by the end of it.

78
00:08:15,174 --> 00:08:16,635
[SPEAKER_00]: But why wait?

79
00:08:16,756 --> 00:08:26,343
[SPEAKER_00]: If he can sort of uproot, overcome, extinguish all the lingering symptoms,

80
00:08:28,008 --> 00:08:35,251
[SPEAKER_00]: From his prior infection with avijja, he should do it.

81
00:08:36,111 --> 00:08:49,055
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it says, Bhikkhu, who is an arahant, with taints destroyed, who has lived a holy life, has done what had to be done, laying down the burden, reached his goal, destroyed the fetters of being.

82
00:08:51,016 --> 00:08:53,457
[SPEAKER_00]: From earth, he directly knows earth.

83
00:08:54,637 --> 00:08:57,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Same, same like an ārya sāvaka, same like a sotāpanna.

84
00:08:58,583 --> 00:09:09,412
[SPEAKER_00]: But he's also liberated from these, from all the fetters, because from Earth, having directly known Earth, he does not conceive it.

85
00:09:09,772 --> 00:09:11,193
[SPEAKER_00]: He does not conceive in Earth.

86
00:09:11,654 --> 00:09:13,175
[SPEAKER_00]: He does not conceive from Earth.

87
00:09:14,496 --> 00:09:20,081
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't conceive Earth is mine and he doesn't delight in it, because he has fully understood it.

88
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:30,804
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there's not much to add really to that, but it's obvious.

89
00:09:31,865 --> 00:09:38,431
[SPEAKER_00]: First, the earth, the phenomenon that has arisen in the experience, needs to be understood.

90
00:09:40,132 --> 00:09:46,759
[SPEAKER_00]: Whether you pin it down to earth, water, fire and air, whether sight, sound, smell, taste, touch, needs to be directly known.

91
00:09:47,459 --> 00:09:49,181
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot be just perceived.

92
00:09:49,441 --> 00:09:51,403
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the important point here.

93
00:09:52,630 --> 00:10:03,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people would often think it's a question of perceiving special things, novelty things, subtle things, and that's how you discover the sub-truths or some hidden realities behind.

94
00:10:04,620 --> 00:10:10,726
[SPEAKER_00]: But it doesn't get more hidden than the fundamental great elements of earth, water, fire and air.

95
00:10:12,107 --> 00:10:17,812
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, even if those things are perceived, ignorance is there, because ignorance is in that perception.

96
00:10:20,987 --> 00:10:39,782
[SPEAKER_00]: So, for an arahant, who has fully understood it, or rather, who is directly... who directly knows it, as the Sutta says, in the same sense Sotāpanna directly knows it, but then arahant also made the effort and extinguished all the symptoms of conceiving, of lingering conceiving, which are kind of gratuitous for an ārya-sāvaka.

97
00:10:41,243 --> 00:10:49,270
[SPEAKER_00]: The main... the main dart of ignorance, the main poisonous sort of infection.

98
00:10:50,180 --> 00:10:50,660
[SPEAKER_00]: That's done.

99
00:10:50,741 --> 00:10:51,461
[SPEAKER_00]: That's uprooted.

100
00:10:52,082 --> 00:10:54,744
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, he just needs to wait for the wound to heal.

101
00:10:55,725 --> 00:10:56,986
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what we would encourage here.

102
00:10:57,807 --> 00:11:04,694
[SPEAKER_00]: In order for a wound of sotāpanna to heal, like the previous wound of being a putujana, he needs to stop conceiving.

103
00:11:05,374 --> 00:11:13,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Make the effort and actually withdraw the conceivings that he might still habitually... well, he will habitually have to a different degree.

104
00:11:15,881 --> 00:11:21,225
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, the second bit is the difference between the three in terms of what they attend to.

105
00:11:21,665 --> 00:11:23,266
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's obviously connected to this, so...

106
00:11:24,767 --> 00:11:38,717
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, because an untaught ordinary person, a patujana, who does not see noble ones and is unskilled and untrained in their dhamma, who does not see good men and is unskilled and undisciplined in their dhamma, does not understand what things should be attended to and what things should not be attended to.

107
00:11:40,063 --> 00:11:46,248
[SPEAKER_00]: Since that is so, he attends to those things he should not attend to, and he does not attend to those things he should attend to.

108
00:11:47,468 --> 00:11:58,797
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... again, so, it's not a question of just, eh... taking for granted what you've been told you must pay attention to, such as bodily sensations and various types of perception.

109
00:11:59,977 --> 00:12:07,523
[SPEAKER_00]: The question is understanding the nature of what should be attended, as in skillful and wholesome.

110
00:12:07,943 --> 00:12:11,804
[SPEAKER_00]: And what should not be attended, as in unskillful, unwholesome.

111
00:12:12,944 --> 00:12:17,025
[SPEAKER_00]: But, in order for... like, you don't perceive, necessarily.

112
00:12:17,145 --> 00:12:22,186
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't perceive skillfulness of a skillful thing, nor unskillfulness of an unskillful thing.

113
00:12:22,346 --> 00:12:23,087
[SPEAKER_00]: You can know it.

114
00:12:25,167 --> 00:12:31,008
[SPEAKER_00]: You can come to those... those... those discernments after... after the effort has been made to be understood.

115
00:12:31,809 --> 00:12:33,989
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, why is a wholesome thing wholesome?

116
00:12:34,029 --> 00:12:37,690
[SPEAKER_00]: And what makes a... sometimes something seemingly unwholesome is actually wholesome.

117
00:12:39,004 --> 00:12:50,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, for somebody who never practiced restraint, saying no to sensual desire and everything can... is very very unpleasant initially, and very painful, and it can seem very counterintuitive as well.

118
00:12:50,752 --> 00:12:55,915
[SPEAKER_00]: It's going against the nature, against the human mind, against the body, against everything.

119
00:12:55,975 --> 00:12:56,815
[SPEAKER_00]: And it is.

120
00:12:56,855 --> 00:12:57,936
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's a wholesome thing.

121
00:12:59,817 --> 00:13:06,762
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, torturing your body, and like extreme forms of asceticism, on a verge of death,

122
00:13:07,597 --> 00:13:10,560
[SPEAKER_00]: Which is like taking that against the body to the extreme.

123
00:13:10,600 --> 00:13:12,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that becomes unwholesome as well.

124
00:13:13,362 --> 00:13:20,648
[SPEAKER_00]: So, things are not necessarily defined by perception, in terms of whether they're wholesome or unwholesome, but that can be understood.

125
00:13:21,649 --> 00:13:22,450
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly it.

126
00:13:22,490 --> 00:13:24,912
[SPEAKER_00]: If one understands that, one can attend to it.

127
00:13:25,673 --> 00:13:27,074
[SPEAKER_00]: One knows what to attend.

128
00:13:29,770 --> 00:13:35,212
[SPEAKER_00]: And as the Buddha said, Putujana does not understand what should be attended to.

129
00:13:36,053 --> 00:13:36,893
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't understand.

130
00:13:37,393 --> 00:13:38,074
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't understand.

131
00:13:38,114 --> 00:13:53,080
[SPEAKER_00]: He might have faith in it, he might have heard an instruction and now he's attending to it, to these things, but even so, if it were to be done rightly, he needs to start developing understanding.

132
00:13:53,100 --> 00:13:58,042
[SPEAKER_00]: And yeah, so he goes on saying, what are the things he should not attend to, that he attends to?

133
00:13:59,386 --> 00:14:07,991
[SPEAKER_00]: There are things such that when he attends to them, the unarisen taint of sensual desire arises in him, and the arisen taint of sensual desire increases.

134
00:14:08,812 --> 00:14:15,095
[SPEAKER_00]: The unarisen taint of being arises in him, and the arisen taint of being increases.

135
00:14:15,255 --> 00:14:21,559
[SPEAKER_00]: The unarisen taint of ignorance arises in him, and the arisen taint of ignorance increases.

136
00:14:21,639 --> 00:14:24,681
[SPEAKER_00]: And obviously, consequently, the things that he should attend to,

137
00:14:25,303 --> 00:14:26,064
[SPEAKER_00]: But he doesn't.

138
00:14:26,784 --> 00:14:35,150
[SPEAKER_00]: Things that, when he attends to them, the unarisen taint of sensuality does not arise, and the arisen taint of sensuality is abandoned.

139
00:14:35,751 --> 00:14:39,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Same goes for the being, the taint of being, and the taint of ignorance.

140
00:14:43,016 --> 00:14:47,860
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he keeps on attending improperly.

141
00:14:47,940 --> 00:14:53,284
[SPEAKER_00]: He keeps... he keeps attending unwisely, so to speak.

142
00:14:55,333 --> 00:14:57,615
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, without a proper perspective.

143
00:14:58,415 --> 00:15:00,176
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he ends up thinking, was I in the past?

144
00:15:00,436 --> 00:15:01,417
[SPEAKER_00]: Was I not in the past?

145
00:15:02,298 --> 00:15:03,258
[SPEAKER_00]: What was I in the past?

146
00:15:03,498 --> 00:15:04,499
[SPEAKER_00]: How was I in the past?

147
00:15:04,679 --> 00:15:06,320
[SPEAKER_00]: Having been what, what did I become?

148
00:15:07,281 --> 00:15:08,302
[SPEAKER_00]: Shall I be in the future?

149
00:15:08,502 --> 00:15:09,643
[SPEAKER_00]: Shall I not be in the future?

150
00:15:10,083 --> 00:15:11,083
[SPEAKER_00]: What shall I be in the future?

151
00:15:11,164 --> 00:15:12,224
[SPEAKER_00]: How shall I be in the future?

152
00:15:12,264 --> 00:15:14,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Having been what, what shall I become in the future?

153
00:15:15,613 --> 00:15:18,194
[SPEAKER_00]: Or else, he is inwardly perplexed about the present.

154
00:15:18,614 --> 00:15:18,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I?

155
00:15:19,775 --> 00:15:20,275
[SPEAKER_00]: Am I not?

156
00:15:20,756 --> 00:15:21,216
[SPEAKER_00]: What am I?

157
00:15:21,536 --> 00:15:22,036
[SPEAKER_00]: How am I?

158
00:15:22,596 --> 00:15:25,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Where has this thing... where has this being come from?

159
00:15:26,278 --> 00:15:26,878
[SPEAKER_00]: Where will it go?

160
00:15:28,459 --> 00:15:33,562
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might notice all these questions have one thing in common, which is they're completely self-centered.

161
00:15:34,402 --> 00:15:38,024
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, obviously, this is the... quite a condensed...

162
00:15:39,620 --> 00:15:40,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Paragraph.

163
00:15:40,120 --> 00:15:45,064
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not... it's not that the person actually sits on the chair and starts thinking in such a crude manner.

164
00:15:45,104 --> 00:15:45,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Was I in the past?

165
00:15:45,964 --> 00:15:46,465
[SPEAKER_00]: Was I not?

166
00:15:47,485 --> 00:15:57,532
[SPEAKER_00]: These questions should be understood on a subtle level of implication, whereby when you're thinking about something in the past or something about the future, you're thinking in a self-centered manner.

167
00:15:57,672 --> 00:16:01,155
[SPEAKER_00]: As in, you don't question your own self in it, in regard to it.

168
00:16:01,235 --> 00:16:03,236
[SPEAKER_00]: You're questioning the content of your experience.

169
00:16:04,899 --> 00:16:09,324
[SPEAKER_00]: You might be even doubting, but what you're not doubting is that there has been some sense of self.

170
00:16:10,386 --> 00:16:12,588
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not doubting that I was in the past.

171
00:16:13,329 --> 00:16:17,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Or rather, you're not doubting the I of the question, was I in the past?

172
00:16:18,155 --> 00:16:21,238
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, was I in the past or was I not in the past?

173
00:16:21,499 --> 00:16:24,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, the question revolves around the self-centered sense of I.

174
00:16:26,013 --> 00:16:35,878
[SPEAKER_00]: And those questions, as the Buddha said, see, there is no, like, answer to them in the sense of, oh, you must figure it out, answer to every individual question here that's been read.

175
00:16:36,298 --> 00:16:50,846
[SPEAKER_00]: On the contrary, these questions arise, or rather, sense of self and self-centeredness arises as a result of improperly attending to sensuality, nature of being, and ignorance.

176
00:16:52,247 --> 00:16:54,448
[SPEAKER_00]: And actually, yeah, here it says, the next paragraph, eh...

177
00:17:17,465 --> 00:17:18,326
[SPEAKER_00]: is the actual truth.

178
00:17:18,466 --> 00:17:21,348
[SPEAKER_00]: My self does not exist as the actual truth.

179
00:17:21,908 --> 00:17:25,551
[SPEAKER_00]: The view I perceive self with self will arise in him.

180
00:17:26,692 --> 00:17:29,854
[SPEAKER_00]: The view that I perceive not-self with self arise in him.

181
00:17:30,475 --> 00:17:33,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Or the view I perceive self with not-self arise in him.

182
00:17:33,997 --> 00:17:40,322
[SPEAKER_00]: And as Jnana-Vedā-Thera pointed out, what Puthujjana cannot develop is the view that I perceive not-self with not-self.

183
00:17:41,063 --> 00:17:44,065
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if that arises in him, then he'll be a sotāpanna.

184
00:17:46,665 --> 00:17:58,675
[SPEAKER_00]: So, he remains fully self-centered and established in his wrong self-view as a result of wrongly attending to these three types of taints, basically.

185
00:17:58,715 --> 00:18:03,079
[SPEAKER_00]: The sensuality, being, nature of phenomena and ignorance.

186
00:18:04,660 --> 00:18:13,488
[SPEAKER_00]: And because he ends up developing self-view, he becomes subjected to aging and death, sorrow, lamentation, pain, grief and despair.

187
00:18:14,329 --> 00:18:14,709
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

188
00:18:17,428 --> 00:18:36,736
[SPEAKER_00]: So, now, a well-taught noble disciple, who does see noble ones, who is skilled and trained in the dhamma, who sees true men and is skilled and trained in the dhamma, understands what things should be attended to and what things should not be attended to.

189
00:18:38,237 --> 00:18:42,959
[SPEAKER_00]: Since that is so, he does not attend to those things he should not attend and he attends to those things he should attend.

190
00:18:44,874 --> 00:18:54,986
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it goes the same, to repeat, what are the things he should not attend are the things that lead to increasing of sensuality, being, and ignorance.

191
00:18:55,327 --> 00:19:02,676
[SPEAKER_00]: And things that are fit for attention are things that lead to decreasing of sensuality, being, and ignorance.

192
00:19:02,936 --> 00:19:04,058
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, because

193
00:19:05,087 --> 00:19:09,631
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he's been attending properly, he attends... he can attend further.

194
00:19:10,492 --> 00:19:12,613
[SPEAKER_00]: This is suffering, he attends properly.

195
00:19:13,394 --> 00:19:15,776
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the origin of suffering, he attends properly.

196
00:19:16,296 --> 00:19:18,698
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the cessation of suffering, he attends properly.

197
00:19:19,239 --> 00:19:24,763
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the way leading to the cessation of suffering, when he attends wisely in this way.

198
00:19:26,525 --> 00:19:28,186
[SPEAKER_00]: These fetters are abandoned in him.

199
00:19:28,546 --> 00:19:32,069
[SPEAKER_00]: Personality view, doubt, and adhering to behavior and duties.

200
00:19:33,090 --> 00:19:33,671
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

201
00:19:40,275 --> 00:19:41,756
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I'm just looking for a Pali word here.

202
00:19:42,576 --> 00:19:59,724
[SPEAKER_00]: It's... yeah, so... this is... this is already... yeah, this is talking about Ārya-sāvaka, who clearly, see, hasn't become a sotāpanna yet, but already learned how to attend to things like an arahant.

203
00:20:00,044 --> 00:20:03,266
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Mūla Pariyāya Sutta 1, Majjhima Nikāva 1 said.

204
00:20:03,286 --> 00:20:04,527
[SPEAKER_00]: By the way, this is Majjhima Nikāva 2.

205
00:20:05,807 --> 00:20:06,467
[SPEAKER_00]: Exit from it.

206
00:20:07,908 --> 00:20:09,049
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in Ārya-sāvaka,

207
00:20:10,025 --> 00:20:36,342
[SPEAKER_00]: By having understood what needs to be attended properly, having understood what proper attention is, having and having having directly known what he's perceiving, then he can practice proper attention in regards to things as they present themselves, and bring the sensuality to decrease, being to decrease, and ignorance to decrease, and then he can also attend in the same manner about the nature of suffering.

208
00:20:39,472 --> 00:20:41,794
[SPEAKER_00]: By doing so, he will become a sotāpanna.

209
00:20:42,775 --> 00:20:54,027
[SPEAKER_00]: He will be free from the self-view, from doubt, and from holding on to virtue and duty or behavior and duties.

210
00:20:58,612 --> 00:20:59,032
[SPEAKER_00]: Then it says,

211
00:21:03,347 --> 00:21:10,113
[SPEAKER_00]: Furthermore, because a noble disciple reflects thus, I'm endowed with the nature of an individual who possesses right view.

212
00:21:11,053 --> 00:21:13,756
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the nature of an individual who possesses right view?

213
00:21:14,416 --> 00:21:17,439
[SPEAKER_00]: This is the nature of the individual who possesses right view.

214
00:21:18,180 --> 00:21:22,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Although he may commit some kind of offense for which a means of rehabilitation has been laid down,

215
00:21:23,708 --> 00:21:29,022
[SPEAKER_00]: Still, he at once confesses, reveals and discloses it to the teacher or to wise companions in the holy life.

216
00:21:29,062 --> 00:21:31,890
[SPEAKER_00]: Having done that, he enters upon restraint for the future.

217
00:21:34,032 --> 00:21:37,853
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, he is his virtue.

218
00:21:38,293 --> 00:21:39,673
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not doing his virtue.

219
00:21:39,693 --> 00:21:40,914
[SPEAKER_00]: He's not keeping his virtue.

220
00:21:40,974 --> 00:21:41,794
[SPEAKER_00]: He has become it.

221
00:21:42,654 --> 00:22:02,879
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why if, say, lapsed in his mindfulness or some old habits that are still strong or something and circumstances press him and he commits some sort of offense for which he needs to go through rehabilitation, he will confess it and reveal it and disclose it to the wise ones, as in to other āryas, to arahants, to his teacher or similar.

222
00:22:03,499 --> 00:22:08,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, eh... he cannot remain concealing it, because he is it.

223
00:22:08,462 --> 00:22:12,004
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not optional for him.

224
00:22:12,104 --> 00:22:27,073
[SPEAKER_00]: And it says, just as a young tender infant, lying prone, once draws back his hand or his foot on a live coal, so too, that is the nature of an individual who possesses the right view.

225
00:22:27,393 --> 00:22:28,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, back to Majjhima Niqab 4.

226
00:22:30,175 --> 00:22:35,987
[SPEAKER_00]: There are Brahmins, some recluses in Brahmins, who perceive day when it is night, and night when it is day.

227
00:22:36,969 --> 00:22:40,857
[SPEAKER_00]: I say that, on their part, this is an abiding in delusion.

228
00:22:41,664 --> 00:22:45,005
[SPEAKER_00]: But I perceive night when it is night, and day when it is day.

229
00:22:45,305 --> 00:22:57,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Rightly speaking, worried to be said of anyone, being not subjected to delusion, has appeared in the world for the welfare and happiness of many, out of compassion for the world, for the good, welfare, happiness of gods and humans.

230
00:22:57,708 --> 00:23:00,249
[SPEAKER_00]: It is me, indeed, that rightly speaking, this should be...

231
00:23:00,429 --> 00:23:03,210
[SPEAKER_00]: It is of me, indeed, that rightly speaking, this should be said.

232
00:23:03,990 --> 00:23:10,192
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this comes back to that, eh... as we open this, the... the perception, eh...

233
00:23:11,984 --> 00:23:21,387
[SPEAKER_00]: Often, you probably... people must have come across pretty much denying what you perceive as a way of dealing, as a way of overcoming.

234
00:23:23,143 --> 00:23:23,783
[SPEAKER_00]: What you perceive.

235
00:23:23,863 --> 00:23:25,784
[SPEAKER_00]: And you will want to overcome it, because you're bothered by it.

236
00:23:26,445 --> 00:23:29,146
[SPEAKER_00]: So, people perceive things that cause them suffering and so on.

237
00:23:29,166 --> 00:23:32,068
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they think that the way out of suffering is to start denying the perception.

238
00:23:32,128 --> 00:23:33,028
[SPEAKER_00]: And you often hear that.

239
00:23:33,528 --> 00:23:37,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Even in the latest schools of Buddhism, people would deny the reality of things.

240
00:23:37,991 --> 00:23:41,292
[SPEAKER_00]: They would say they're constantly changing on a subatomic level and whatnot.

241
00:23:41,332 --> 00:23:52,038
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what you're looking at, it's not really a real thing, which is just another word... another way of sort of veiling things in a metaphysical ignorance of... of Maya and all Hindu's teachings.

242
00:23:52,818 --> 00:23:56,340
[SPEAKER_00]: whereby the the reality is hidden behind the appearances.

243
00:23:57,481 --> 00:24:02,604
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, the Buddha says, well, that's... whichever way you take that, it's going to be abiding in delusion.

244
00:24:03,165 --> 00:24:04,826
[SPEAKER_00]: When there is night, he perceives night.

245
00:24:04,966 --> 00:24:08,048
[SPEAKER_00]: Means, what he perceives, he sees that as a real thing.

246
00:24:08,834 --> 00:24:10,876
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there was never a problem to begin with.

247
00:24:11,757 --> 00:24:26,953
[SPEAKER_00]: As again, Majjhima Nikāh 1, Mūla Parīyā, as we read in the beginning, it's not the perceiving that's the... well, sorry, it's not what you perceive that's the problem, the problem is in your act of perceiving, that misconceives what's being perceived.

248
00:24:28,854 --> 00:24:36,562
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you can try and examine and investigate as much as you want the content and the molecular compounds of the contents of your perception.

249
00:24:37,162 --> 00:24:42,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Still, that's not bringing you an inch closer to understanding the nature of perception, which is see...

250
00:24:42,688 --> 00:24:49,374
[SPEAKER_00]: In order to understand the nature of perception, you need to be... you need to stop being concerned with the content of your perception.

251
00:24:50,035 --> 00:24:52,857
[SPEAKER_00]: So, first, you restrain the unwholesome content.

252
00:24:53,578 --> 00:25:00,620
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you overcome the wholesome content through discerning the nature of what you're perceiving, not what you're perceiving.

253
00:25:04,521 --> 00:25:06,501
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there was... this was Majjhima 4.

254
00:25:31,598 --> 00:25:32,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, yeah, here is an interesting passage.

255
00:25:34,960 --> 00:25:39,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite useful to remind yourself of it.

256
00:25:39,283 --> 00:25:40,363
[SPEAKER_00]: Majjhima Nikaya 8.

257
00:25:42,644 --> 00:25:43,085
[SPEAKER_00]: Cunda.

258
00:25:43,565 --> 00:25:51,029
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea that one who has himself sunk into the mud can pull out another who has sunk into the mud.

259
00:25:51,650 --> 00:25:52,550
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

260
00:25:53,811 --> 00:25:55,012
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's quite obvious.

261
00:25:56,242 --> 00:26:11,800
[SPEAKER_00]: Unless a person is at least a sotāpanna, as in somebody who is pertained to the direct knowledge of an arahant, as Mūla Parīyāya Sūtra said above, unless somebody has developed a right view, they cannot teach somebody else.

262
00:26:12,501 --> 00:26:16,826
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, puthujjana cannot lead another puthujjana out of a state of puthujjana.

263
00:26:18,670 --> 00:26:25,795
[SPEAKER_00]: A person who is himself deep sinking into the mud, cannot take another one, who is also in that mud right next to him.

264
00:26:25,915 --> 00:26:27,156
[SPEAKER_00]: They will be sinking together.

265
00:26:27,917 --> 00:26:43,529
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's important to maintain that criteria, because people tend to water it down through sort of an attitude of, well, I am a putujana, but for as long as I'm making this effort,

266
00:26:44,863 --> 00:26:46,164
[SPEAKER_00]: My intentions are good.

267
00:26:46,404 --> 00:26:47,424
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't mean any harm.

268
00:26:47,964 --> 00:26:50,385
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that means I can help others.

269
00:26:50,405 --> 00:26:54,106
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you can help others in a very, very relative sense.

270
00:26:54,246 --> 00:26:55,987
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe point them to the suttas and so on.

271
00:26:56,427 --> 00:27:07,231
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you actually think you can teach others in a way of... direct way out of suffering, without you yourself understanding the way out of suffering, that's a complete contradiction.

272
00:27:07,771 --> 00:27:13,293
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the best way to help others, the most compassionate way to help others, is to free yourself first.

273
00:27:14,412 --> 00:27:19,914
[SPEAKER_00]: To recognize that first you need to step up, find a way out of the mud, the sinking mud.

274
00:27:21,575 --> 00:27:23,676
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you do, then you will help others.

275
00:27:23,696 --> 00:27:25,477
[SPEAKER_00]: You will know exactly how to help others.

276
00:27:26,077 --> 00:27:29,998
[SPEAKER_00]: But then you might realize that some of them don't want to be helped.

277
00:27:30,699 --> 00:27:36,541
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the idea that one who has not sunk into the mud can pull out another who has sunk into the mud, that's possible.

278
00:27:37,862 --> 00:27:43,244
[SPEAKER_00]: The idea that one who himself is unextinguished, untrained,

279
00:27:43,993 --> 00:27:47,788
[SPEAKER_00]: Can help somebody else to become tame, trained and extinguished.

280
00:27:47,828 --> 00:27:48,571
[SPEAKER_00]: That's impossible.

